{
  "image_name": "2c4cf4b5eec1a8b44bd6cb9b9eefc69c.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how estrogen-related signaling affects several nonreproductive cell types and ovarian follicle cells, with outcomes that depend strongly on cell context. Across the panels, estradiol (E2) acts through estrogen receptors at the membrane and in the nucleus, engaging kinase pathways and transcriptional programs that alter metabolism, growth, and differentiation.\n\nIn breast cancer cells, the image contrasts high- versus low-glucose conditions: under high glucose, estrogen appears to promote glycolysis, lactate production, ATP generation, and ultimately cell viability and proliferation, whereas under low glucose it shifts metabolism toward pyruvate entry into the TCA cycle and oxidative phosphorylation through acetyl-CoA production.\n\nIn the heart, estrogen signaling is shown in both cardiomyocytes and myofibroblasts. In cardiomyocytes, it modulates calcium- and kinase-dependent pathways, histone deacetylases, and transcription factors such as MEF2 and NFAT, converging on hypertrophic gene expression. In myofibroblasts, it seems to restrain profibrotic signaling linked to TGFβ, JNK, and SMAD2/3, reducing fibrotic gene expression.\n\nThe lower panels depict vascular and ovarian effects. In endothelial cells, estrogen and LXR-related signals increase eNOS activity and expression, boosting nitric oxide and supporting artery relaxation and re-endothelialization. In granulosa cells, androgen/estrogen-linked EGFR and FSH receptor signaling promotes follicle growth and suppresses apoptosis.",
